### Health — /health
#
# The split between these two is the design decision, not the endpoints themselves.

### Liveness — 200 whenever the process is up, with an empty checks array.
# It runs no dependency checks at all. A liveness probe that reached PostgreSQL would mean a
# database outage causes Kubernetes to restart every replica in a loop, which cannot help and
# actively hinders recovery by hammering the database with reconnections as each pod starts.
GET {{host}}/health/live

### Readiness — 200 when the dependencies needed to serve traffic are reachable, 503 when not.
# Two checks, weighted differently. PostgreSQL failing makes this Unhealthy, because the API
# genuinely cannot serve without it. Redis failing reports Degraded — still a 200 — because it is a
# cache in front of the source of truth: an instance that cannot reach it answers every endpoint
# correctly, only more slowly.
#
# To see the difference, stop the Redis container and call this again: still 200, with redis
# reported as Degraded and the API still serving.
GET {{host}}/health/ready
